{"answer":"No. The claimed conclusion is false: √2 is irrational. The outlined argument becomes valid only after justifying that an integer with even square is even; then a lowest-terms representation would force both numerator and denominator to be divisible by 2, a contradiction.","approaches":[{"description":"Use reductio: assume √2 is rational, choose a coprime integer representation, and use an explicit odd-square calculation to force both integers to be even.","id":"direct-reductio","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This directly addresses the missing parity justification and yields a contradiction with coprimality."}],"change_log":["Retained the audited reductio argument but made the parity implication explicit for both a and b.","Named and explicitly applied the reduced-fraction representation theorem, including its hypothesis.","Recorded the rationality assumption as local and its discharge at the contradiction step.","No audit objection remains unresolved; no external sources or computational checks were supplied or used."],"claims":[{"basis":"local_assumption","basis_reference":"Temporary assumption for reductio.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":["step-contradiction"],"id":"claim-rational-assumption","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":["step-lowest-terms","step-square","step-a-square-even","step-a-even","step-b-square-even","step-b-even","step-contradiction"],"statement":"Assume, for contradiction, that √2 is rational.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"standard_result","basis_reference":"Reduced-fraction representation theorem: every rational number r can be written r=a/b for integers a,b with b ≠ 0 and gcd(a,b)=1. Its hypothesis is claim-rational-assumption, and it is applied in step-lowest-terms.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-rational-assumption"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claim-lowest-terms","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, there are integers a,b with b ≠ 0, √2 = a/b, and gcd(a,b)=1.","step_ids":["step-lowest-terms"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the arithmetic and parity arguments in step-a-even and step-b-even.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-lowest-terms"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claim-both-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The integers a and b are both even.","step_ids":["step-a-even","step-b-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The contradiction in step-contradiction discharges the local rationality assumption.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-rational-assumption","claim-lowest-terms","claim-both-even"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claim-irrational","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"√2 is irrational.","step_ids":["step-contradiction"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-lowest-terms","justification":"Apply the reduced-fraction representation theorem to the temporary rationality assumption.","statement":"Choose integers a,b with b ≠ 0, √2 = a/b, and gcd(a,b)=1."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-lowest-terms"],"id":"step-square","justification":"Because b ≠ 0, square √2 = a/b and multiply by b².","statement":"a² = 2b²."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-square"],"id":"step-a-square-even","justification":"The right-hand side 2b² is divisible by 2.","statement":"a² is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-a-square-even"],"id":"step-a-even","justification":"If a were odd, then a=2k+1 for an integer k, so a²=4k²+4k+1=2(2k²+2k)+1 would be odd, contradicting step-a-square-even.","statement":"a is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-square","step-a-even"],"id":"step-b-square-even","justification":"By step-a-even, a=2m for some integer m. Substitution in step-square gives 4m²=2b²; dividing this equality by 2 gives b²=2m².","statement":"b² is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-b-square-even"],"id":"step-b-even","justification":"If b were odd, the same calculation as in step-a-even would make b² odd, contradicting step-b-square-even.","statement":"b is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-lowest-terms","step-a-even","step-b-even"],"id":"step-contradiction","justification":"Steps step-a-even and step-b-even show that 2 divides both a and b, contradicting gcd(a,b)=1 from step-lowest-terms. This contradiction discharges the temporary assumption in claim-rational-assumption.","statement":"The temporary assumption that √2 is rational is false; therefore √2 is irrational."}],"question_status":"refuted","tool_requests":[]}
